From 093b69decee608a5f922d97c8ad5afb832e9b8e5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Allen Gregory <allengregoryiv@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:55:14 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Native camera: reconnect after a USB reset or re-enumeration;
 reopened handles get the pipeline's NAPA settings

NativeFrameProvider: when the native thread gives the device up (cameraStats[5]: ENODEV/EIO, or no frame for
SPECTRUM_NATIVE_FRAME_TIMEOUT_MS, default 500) the handle is closed and the camera reopened by its by-id path every
100 ms (whatever /dev/videoN it comes back as) in the selected mode, controls written back where they differ
(NativeCameraSettables.restoreControls); reconnects are logged, health state disconnected/recovering, recoveries
counts them. A reconnect never falls back to cscore.

NapaDetectorJNI.cameraOpens: a reopened camera's NAPA handle is often the closed one's address again, so
NapaDetector skipped re-sending the pipeline's NAPA settings (the new detector ran NAPA defaults instead of min tag
22: Swyft ~85 fps at ~40 ms after a reconnect or handover) and NativePublish missed the reset of its counters.
Both now compare (handle, opens).

Native side (SpectrumJetson detector/napa/NapaDetectorJNI.cc, patches/photonvision-137): device loss detection,
no publish of frames whose buffer never completed (the Thriftiest's 20 fps of one stale frame), the device let go
at once (a held fd made a uvcvideo rebind come back without a video node).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
